<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramiz Alia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/2011">2011</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramiz Alia was the communist leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a> as first secretary of the Party of Labour in 1985. Facing economic crisis, student protests and mass flight abroad, he allowed opposition parties in December 1990. He was elected president under the new multi-party system in 1991 but resigned in 1992 after the Democratic Party won the elections.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramiz-alia.jpg" alt="Ramiz Alia" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albanian State · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramiz_Alia_(portret).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramiz Alia was born on 18 October 1925 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> to Gheg Albanian parents who were Muslim and had fled persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. He grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a>, joined the underground Albanian Communist Youth Organisation in 1941 and became a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/party-of-labour-of-albania">Albanian Communist Party</a> in 1943.</p><p>He rose rapidly under the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>. He was elected to the Central Committee in November 1948, became a candidate member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> in 1956 and a full member by 1961, and was the party's chief ideologist in social and cultural fields. Hoxha named no official successor, but there were clear signs that he favoured Alia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Alia was the second and last leader of the People's Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, serving as First Secretary of the Party of Labour from 1985 to 1991. He was also head of state from 1982 to 1992.</p><p>Alia tried to change the system on a limited, cautious basis, but reform from above threatened to become reform from below, largely because of the demands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>'s youth. On 9 December 1990 students marched in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a> demanding reforms, and Alia met them and agreed to further steps towards democratisation. On 12 December 1990 he signed a law allowing political pluralism, which he later called a failure.</p><p>In the 1991 elections, the first democratic ones in the country, he ran for parliament in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a> constituency and was defeated by the mining engineer Franko Kroqi, a candidate of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-of-albania">Democratic Party of Albania</a>. After that loss and the Democratic Party's landslide in the spring 1992 general election, he resigned as president on 3 April 1992. On 9 April the People's Assembly elected the party's leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sali-berisha">Sali Berisha</a>, as head of state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alia was placed under house arrest in August 1992 and imprisoned in August 1993. In May 1994 he went on trial with other senior officials for abuse of power and misappropriation of state funds and received a nine-year sentence, later cut on appeal. He was freed on 7 July 1995 and charged again in 1996 with crimes against humanity. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a> on 7 October 2011.</p><p>His legacy remains polarising. Some dissidents accuse him of continuing Hoxha's repressive practices, while others praise him for dissolving the Party of Labour's monopoly peacefully and avoiding the bloodshed seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramiz Alia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Hoxha died in 1985, <b>Ramiz Alia</b> allowed cautious change.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-socialist-republic-of-albania">People's Socialist Republic of Albania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.07,19.51&amp;year=1980" aria-label="Shkodër on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5024 666 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ramiz Alia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5474 936h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5474 936h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.07,19.51&amp;year=1980">What was here in 1980? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
